===Second attempt (15 February)===

The date for the second election was set for 15 February 2008. The second election also allowed for three rounds, with the same rules as the first election, and both candidates ran again. In the second election, KSČM proposed an additional candidate: [[Member of the European Parliament|MEP]] and former TV news presenter [[Jana Bobošíková]], known as an outspoken critic of the EU and the planned US missile shield in Poland and the Czech Republic.<ref name=IHT2000812>{{Cite web | title = Czech Communists name EU parliament deputy as presidential candidate |work=International Herald Tribune | url= http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2008/02/12/europe/EU-POL-Czech-Presidential-Elections.php | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref>

The three nominees were Švejnar, Klaus and Bobošíková. Bobošíková was nominated by 17 KSČM deputies, but the Communists indicated that they would be inclined to support Švejnar under certain conditions.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech MEP sees her chance of being elected president as small |work=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=296670 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech Communists have three conditions for supporting Svejnar |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=296569 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref>

Klaus's chances of reelection were boosted when Snítilý announced that he would vote for Klaus prior to the session.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Klaus's election chances grow, Czech MP Snítilý will support him |work=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=296996 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> In his address on 15 February 2008 before the joint session of parliament, Klaus unexpectedly declared that he supported holding the presidential election as a public ballot, and not a secret ballot as he had demanded before.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech president in favor of public presidential election |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url= http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297023 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> Klaus's speech was also significantly more nationalist and eurosceptic than the previous week. This was interpreted as a sign that he knew he had the votes to win and no longer needed to moderate his words.

Bobošíková withdrew her candidacy shortly after the debate and before the first round of voting, citing a lack of support.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Bobosikova withdraws Czech presidential candidacy |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url= http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297082 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> The Communists then said they would use the same strategy as in the first election, supporting Švejnar in the first and second rounds, but abstaining in the third, thus attempting again to prevent the session from electing a president.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech Communists seek three-round presidential election |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297094 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref>

After further debate on the method of voting, ODS agreed to hold the vote again with a public ballot.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech Civic Democrats accept open presidential election |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297098 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> Green MP [[Olga Zubová]] was absent from the session for health reasons.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Communists, Social Democrats at odds over Czech presidential election |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url= http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297113 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref>

In the first round of voting, Klaus received 141 votes, just enough for his reelection in the third round.<ref>{{Cite web | title = MPs do not pick president in first round, Klaus, Svejnar advance |work=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url= http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297121 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> The second round saw similar results, but also a drop in support for Švejnar from the Communists.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech MPs elect no president in 2nd round, Klaus, Svejnar advance |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297134 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref> Prior to the third round, it was reported that the Minister for Human Rights and Minorities [[Džamila Stehlíková]] from the Greens was at Zubová's residence, in an attempt to convince her to attend the third round of voting to improve Švejnar's chances.<ref>{{Cite web | title = Czech Greens minister leaves for ill deputy Zubova |publisher=ČeskéNoviny.cz | url=http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/index_view.php?id=297136 | accessdate = 20 February 2008 | year = 2008 }}</ref>

In the third round Klaus was re-elected as President of the Czech Republic.

